<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.10.4: Rewriting Expressions in Vertex Form</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02503</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.10.4: Rewriting Expressions in Vertex Form</md:title>
    <md:uuid>425acaa9-9688-43f0-b43d-f5c5fbe67989</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here is one way to rewrite \(3x^2+12x+9\) in <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">vertex form (of a quadratic expression)</term>.</item>
</list>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Study the steps and write a brief explanation of what is happening at each step.</para>
    <para>Original expression</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - \(3(x^2+4x+3)\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - \(3(x^2+4x+4+3−4)\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - \(3(x^2+4x+4−1)\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - \(3((x+2)^2−1)\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - \(3(x+2)^2−3\)</para>
  <para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
  <para>Original expression</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Rewrite the expression with 3 as a factor.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Add 4 to complete the square.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms so we can see the perfect square, \(x^2+4x+4\).</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Rewrite \(x^2+4x+4\) as a squared expression.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Apply the distributive property to put the expression in vertex form.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="b71640c3-23f2-4186-98e0-dd901768db6b" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q1a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="94449039-e496-4222-9357-adb316d75f6e" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-2">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">vertex (of a graph)</term> that represents this expression?</para>
      <para>Enter the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex. </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is -2.</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q1b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="f18e0bfe-9bca-49e0-9491-9ba2737676f3" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-3">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents this expression?</para>
      <para>Enter the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex. </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is -3.</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </div>
</div>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Does the graph open upward or downward? Explain how you know.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para> The graph opens upward. For example, in an earlier unit, we saw that when the coefficient of the squared term
    \((x−h)^2\) is positive, the graph opens upward.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Rewrite \(-2x^2−4x+6\) in vertex form.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(-2(x+1)^2+8\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Rewrite \(4x^2+24x+20\) in vertex form.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(4(x+3)^2−16\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Rewrite \(-x^2+20x\) in vertex form.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(-(x−10)^2+100\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
